WEBVTT

00:00:01.617 --> 00:00:04.468
Penguin readers, level one.

00:00:04.528 --> 00:00:08.430
The Missing Coins by John Escott.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.000
Bath is a very old city in England.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.761
It has a beautiful abbey.

00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:22.282
A lot of people visit the city every year.

00:00:22.342 --> 00:00:28.716
And in the summer, there are always a lot of tourists in the streets.

00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:33.670
Pete and Carla are students in Bath.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.040
They like the city.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.582
Pete likes looking at the beautiful old buildings.

00:00:40.642 --> 00:00:44.923
Carla likes walking in the little streets.

00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:50.424
She often finds pretty little shops there and goes into them.

00:00:50.484 --> 00:00:57.332
Or she sits with Pete in a cafe and watches the tourists.

00:00:57.392 --> 00:01:03.390
It's a Saturday in summer and a hot afternoon.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.432
There are a lot of tourists in the city today.

00:01:07.492 --> 00:01:12.516
People from many countries are going into the Abbey.

00:01:12.575 --> 00:01:17.839
Let's find a quiet street away from the tourists.

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:19.520
Carla says.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.712
Okay, Pete says.

00:01:22.772 --> 00:01:25.594
He likes being with Carla.

00:01:25.654 --> 00:01:29.156
They walk away from the Abbey.

00:01:29.216 --> 00:01:35.040
After some time, they are in a little street behind the Abbey.

00:01:35.099 --> 00:01:38.741
This is pretty, Carla says.

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:43.472
There are only two or three tourists in this street.

00:01:43.532 --> 00:01:46.414
The shops have small windows.

00:01:46.474 --> 00:01:50.365
Some houses have pretty little gardens.

00:01:50.425 --> 00:01:56.384
It's quiet here, but Pete and Carla can hear music.

00:01:56.444 --> 00:01:59.262
Look, Pete says.

00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:02.324
That man is playing a flute.

00:02:02.384 --> 00:02:04.946
Let's go and listen.

00:02:05.006 --> 00:02:09.570
They walk across the street and watch the man.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.872
Four or five people are listening.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.404
Why is he playing here?

00:02:15.464 --> 00:02:17.046
Carla asks.

00:02:17.106 --> 00:02:20.598
There aren't many tourists in this street.

00:02:20.658 --> 00:02:23.370
No, Pete says.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:28.019
And there are only three or four coins in his hat.

00:02:28.078 --> 00:02:32.445
Pete and Carla listen to the flute player.

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:35.796
The tune is Greensleeves.

00:02:35.856 --> 00:02:39.547
I know that tune, Carla says.

00:02:39.607 --> 00:02:43.048
And she puts a coin in the man's hat.

00:02:43.108 --> 00:02:45.709
Pete takes her hand.

00:02:45.768 --> 00:02:49.690
Let's go, Carla, he says to her.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.191
Let's see the shops in the next street.

00:02:53.251 --> 00:02:58.271
Okay, Carla says, they walk away.

00:02:58.331 --> 00:03:05.573
The flute player watches them, but his tune, Greensleeves, doesn't stop.

00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:13.456
He isn't a very good flute player, Pete says, but it's a good tune.

00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:18.690
There were only four coins in his hat, Carla says.

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:23.150
That's because he isn't very good, Pete says, 

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.548
and he's playing in the wrong street.

00:03:26.608 --> 00:03:30.116
The tourists are near the Abbey.

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:33.788
They walk to the next street.

00:03:33.848 --> 00:03:37.492
They find a little shop there.

00:03:37.552 --> 00:03:42.116
There are some old coins and stamps in the shop window.

00:03:42.176 --> 00:03:45.258
They come from many countries.

00:03:45.318 --> 00:03:48.670
Let's go in here, Carla says.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.514
It's an interesting little shop.

00:03:51.574 --> 00:03:54.704
Okay, Pete says.

00:03:54.764 --> 00:03:57.644
The shop is small.

00:03:57.704 --> 00:04:03.396
Pete and Carla go in and an old man watches them.

00:04:03.456 --> 00:04:06.638
He is the shopkeeper.

00:04:06.698 --> 00:04:10.680
Good afternoon, he says.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.342
Good afternoon, Carla says.

00:04:13.362 --> 00:04:16.724
Hi, Pete says.

00:04:16.784 --> 00:04:19.306
What can I do for you?

00:04:19.366 --> 00:04:21.476
The shopkeeper says.

00:04:21.536 --> 00:04:25.656
Can we look at the stamps? Pete asks.
 
00:04:25.716 --> 00:04:28.704
The shopkeeper looks at them.

00:04:28.763 --> 00:04:31.816
Okay, he says.

00:04:31.876 --> 00:04:35.218
Do you collect stamps?

00:04:35.278 --> 00:04:40.873
No, but I'm going to start collecting them, Pete says.

00:04:40.933 --> 00:04:43.555
They're interesting.

00:04:43.615 --> 00:04:48.388
Yes, they are, the shopkeeper says.

00:04:48.448 --> 00:04:54.920
Pete looks at some stamps from Canada and some stamps from South America.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.422
These are beautiful stamps, he says.

00:04:59.482 --> 00:05:04.468
The shopkeeper stands near Pete and watches him.

00:05:04.528 --> 00:05:09.824
He isn't happy, Carla thinks. Why?
 
00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:13.407
We aren't going to steal his stamps.

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:16.852
She looks at some coins.

00:05:16.912 --> 00:05:19.706
Are these coins very old?

00:05:19.766 --> 00:05:22.278
Carla asks the old man.

00:05:22.338 --> 00:05:27.291
They're 300 years old, says the shopkeeper.

00:05:27.351 --> 00:05:29.823
They're very valuable.

00:05:29.882 --> 00:05:34.125
Every coin in my shop is valuable.

00:05:34.185 --> 00:05:37.768
A girl is working in the shop too.

00:05:37.828 --> 00:05:40.219
She looks at Carla.

00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:45.862
Carla smiles, but the girl doesn't smile at her.

00:05:45.922 --> 00:05:50.584
She isn't very friendly, Carla thinks.

00:05:50.644 --> 00:05:56.095
Suddenly they hear a telephone in the room behind the shop.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.756
The shopkeeper doesn't answer it for a minute.

00:05:59.816 --> 00:06:04.907
Then he looks at the girl and she looks at him.

00:06:04.967 --> 00:06:09.528
They don't speak, but Carla understands.

00:06:09.588 --> 00:06:17.436
The shopkeeper doesn't speak, but he is saying to the girl, watch these students.

00:06:17.496 --> 00:06:21.800
Excuse me, he says to Carla and Pete, 

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.398
and he goes into the back room.

00:06:25.458 --> 00:06:29.372
Pete is looking at the stamps.

00:06:29.432 --> 00:06:33.614
Come and see these, Carla, he says.

00:06:33.674 --> 00:06:36.508
They're from South America.

00:06:36.568 --> 00:06:39.930
I want to start collecting them.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:44.640
But Carla doesn't like the people in this shop.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:50.622
They aren't very friendly and she wants to look at the city.

00:06:50.682 --> 00:06:54.736
They're very expensive, she says.

00:06:54.796 --> 00:06:57.198
Let's go Pete.

00:06:57.258 --> 00:07:02.222
The girl watches them and then she smiles.

00:07:02.282 --> 00:07:06.456
She's happy because we're going, Carla thinks.

00:07:06.516 --> 00:07:08.476
But why?

00:07:08.536 --> 00:07:14.048
Pete and Carla go back into the big streets with the tourists.

00:07:14.107 --> 00:07:18.840
Carla is happy now because they are not in the shop.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.102
But the sun is hot and Pete wants a drink.

00:07:24.162 --> 00:07:28.924
Let's go to a cafe near the Abbey, he says.

00:07:28.984 --> 00:07:34.728
He wanted the stamps from South America, but he doesn't say this.

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:38.349
There are tables outside the cafe.

00:07:38.409 --> 00:07:41.260
Pete and Carla sit down.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.972
There are a lot of people in the cafe.

00:07:45.032 --> 00:07:48.203
Can we go into the Abbey next?

00:07:48.262 --> 00:07:50.084
Carla asks.

00:07:50.144 --> 00:07:54.545
He wants the stamps from South America, she thinks.

00:07:54.605 --> 00:07:57.436
But they're very expensive.

00:07:57.496 --> 00:08:03.254
I want a drink first, Pete says. Okay.
 
00:08:03.314 --> 00:08:06.572
Carla says, and she smiles at him.

00:08:06.632 --> 00:08:09.924
Pete smiles at her, too.

00:08:09.984 --> 00:08:16.438
After a minute or two, they hear, hey, there you are!

00:08:16.498 --> 00:08:19.110
Pete and Carla look up.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.122
It's the old man from the shop.

00:08:22.182 --> 00:08:24.164
What does he want?

00:08:24.224 --> 00:08:25.614
Pete says.

00:08:25.674 --> 00:08:28.446
I don't know, Carla says.

00:08:28.506 --> 00:08:30.198
But he's angry.

00:08:30.258 --> 00:08:32.180
What's wrong?

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.573
The shopkeeper comes to the cafe.

00:08:35.633 --> 00:08:38.044
He is very angry.

00:08:38.105 --> 00:08:41.857
Where are my coins? He says.
 
00:08:41.918 --> 00:08:44.709
Your coins? Carla says.
 
00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:46.912
I don't understand.

00:08:46.972 --> 00:08:48.762
They're missing.

00:08:48.822 --> 00:08:50.794
The shopkeeper says.

00:08:50.854 --> 00:08:53.486
Did you steal them?

00:08:53.546 --> 00:08:56.898
Carla and Pete are surprised.

00:08:56.958 --> 00:09:01.484
The people at the tables near them are surprised too.

00:09:01.544 --> 00:09:05.650
They watch the two friends and the old man.

00:09:05.709 --> 00:09:08.142
Steal your coins?

00:09:08.202 --> 00:09:10.704
No, Carla says.

00:09:10.764 --> 00:09:13.706
Her face is hot and red.

00:09:13.766 --> 00:09:18.980
We didn't steal any coins or stamps, Pete says.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:20.993
What are you saying?

00:09:21.052 --> 00:09:23.739
He is angry now.

00:09:23.799 --> 00:09:28.528
You've got my coins, the shopkeeper says.

00:09:28.588 --> 00:09:33.884
The coins are missing and this isn't the first time.

00:09:33.944 --> 00:09:36.996
Some coins were missing last week.

00:09:37.056 --> 00:09:41.220
Some students had them, too, I think.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.864
Did you see the students with the coins?

00:09:44.924 --> 00:09:46.486
Pete says.

00:09:46.546 --> 00:09:50.064
The old man doesn't answer.

00:09:50.124 --> 00:09:54.604
A policeman walks down the street to the cafe.

00:09:54.664 --> 00:09:58.690
He watches Carla and Pete and the shopkeeper.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:03.204
Then he says, what's wrong?

00:10:03.264 --> 00:10:09.205
These two students came into my shop, the shopkeeper says.

00:10:09.265 --> 00:10:13.716
I went to the back room and answered the telephone.

00:10:13.776 --> 00:10:17.158
Then I went back into the shop.

00:10:17.218 --> 00:10:19.800
Some valuable coins were missing 

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:24.150
and the two students were not in the shop.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:28.411
The policeman looks at Pete and Carla.

00:10:28.472 --> 00:10:32.227
What do you say? He asks.
 
00:10:32.287 --> 00:10:36.825
Yes, we went into the shop, Carla says.

00:10:36.886 --> 00:10:39.938
We wanted to look at the stamps.

00:10:39.998 --> 00:10:44.404
But we didn't steal them, Pete says.

00:10:44.464 --> 00:10:47.919
And we didn't take the coins.

00:10:47.979 --> 00:10:53.700
Were there many people in the shop? the policeman asks. 

00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:00.950
No, the shopkeeper says. Only these two students, and Tracy. 

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:06.100
Tracy works in my shop. She doesn't steal coins. 

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.850
How do you know? asks the policeman. 

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:20.874
I looked in her bag and pockets today, the shopkeeper says, and I looked in them last week.

00:11:20.934 --> 00:11:25.074
You can look in my bag, Carla says.

00:11:25.134 --> 00:11:30.126
And you can look in my pockets, Pete says.

00:11:30.185 --> 00:11:33.186
The shopkeeper is surprised.

00:11:33.246 --> 00:11:36.137
Can I? He says.
 
00:11:36.197 --> 00:11:38.487
Please look.

00:11:38.547 --> 00:11:42.122
Please look now, Carla says.

00:11:42.182 --> 00:11:45.106
We didn't steal any coins.

00:11:45.166 --> 00:11:47.918
She gives him her bag.

00:11:47.978 --> 00:11:53.852
The shopkeeper looks in Carla's bag and in their pockets.

00:11:53.911 --> 00:11:57.594
The people at the tables near them watch him.

00:11:57.654 --> 00:12:01.536
Is he going to find the coins, they think?

00:12:01.596 --> 00:12:05.957
But he finds no coins or stamps.

00:12:06.017 --> 00:12:10.988
We didn't steal the coins, Pete says.

00:12:11.048 --> 00:12:15.810
And now you know that.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:19.331
I'm sorry, the shopkeeper says.

00:12:19.391 --> 00:12:24.163
I was wrong, but I don't understand it.

00:12:24.223 --> 00:12:27.234
Where are the coins?

00:12:27.294 --> 00:12:31.616
Then the old man goes back to his shop.

00:12:31.676 --> 00:12:37.233
He isn't very happy, Pete says to the policeman.

00:12:37.293 --> 00:12:42.510
Coins are very small, the policeman says.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:47.721
Perhaps they're in the shop, but he can't see them.

00:12:47.781 --> 00:12:51.882
Some young people start to play music.

00:12:51.942 --> 00:12:54.703
A girl plays a flute.

00:12:54.763 --> 00:13:00.515
We didn't steal any coins, Carla says to the policeman.

00:13:00.575 --> 00:13:04.406
I know, the policeman says.

00:13:04.466 --> 00:13:07.782
Carla listens to the music.

00:13:07.842 --> 00:13:10.498
It is Greensleeves.

00:13:10.558 --> 00:13:14.930
Where did I hear that? She thinks.
 
00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:20.983
And then she remembers the flute player near the coin and stamp shop.

00:13:21.043 --> 00:13:25.066
He played that tune, she remembers.

00:13:25.126 --> 00:13:28.586
Pete is watching her face.

00:13:28.647 --> 00:13:31.808
What is it? He asks her.
 
00:13:31.868 --> 00:13:33.866
What's wrong?

00:13:33.926 --> 00:13:39.718
I know now, she says, the missing coins.

00:13:39.778 --> 00:13:42.150
I know the answer.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:45.034
Do you? Pete says.
 
00:13:45.094 --> 00:13:46.815
What is it?

00:13:46.875 --> 00:13:51.490
Come with me, she says to Pete and the policeman.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:55.453
We're going to find the man with the flute.

00:13:55.513 --> 00:13:57.616
Which man?

00:13:57.676 --> 00:13:59.858
The policeman says.

00:13:59.918 --> 00:14:05.743
He plays in the street near the old man's shop, Pete says.

00:14:05.803 --> 00:14:12.759
Then Carla, Pete and the policeman run to the stamp and coin shop.

00:14:12.819 --> 00:14:16.743
Hey, you, Carla says.

00:14:16.802 --> 00:14:20.200
The flute player is going to walk away, 

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:23.158
but the policeman stops him.

00:14:23.218 --> 00:14:26.120
Wait, the policeman says.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.970
We want to speak to you.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:32.130
The man wants to run now.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:36.424
I'm going home, he says.

00:14:36.484 --> 00:14:40.526
No, wait, the policeman says.

00:14:40.586 --> 00:14:43.269
He looks at Carla.

00:14:43.329 --> 00:14:48.353
You have the coins, Carla says to the flute player.

00:14:48.414 --> 00:14:51.246
No, the man says.

00:14:51.306 --> 00:14:56.702
He starts to run away, but the policeman stops him again.

00:14:56.762 --> 00:15:01.177
How did he do it? Pete asks Carla.
 
00:15:01.237 --> 00:15:03.818
It was the girl in the shop.

00:15:03.878 --> 00:15:05.579
Carla says.

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:08.190
We looked at some stamps.

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:09.631
Remember?

00:15:09.691 --> 00:15:15.214
And the shopkeeper was on the telephone in the room at the back.

00:15:15.274 --> 00:15:19.806
But how did the flute player get the coins?

00:15:19.866 --> 00:15:21.766
The policeman asks.

00:15:21.826 --> 00:15:24.438
Yes, Pete says.

00:15:24.498 --> 00:15:27.203
I don't understand.

00:15:27.263 --> 00:15:31.568
He was outside the window, Carla says.

00:15:31.628 --> 00:15:35.830
The girl went to the window with the coins.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:40.992
But there were people in the street, Pete says.

00:15:41.052 --> 00:15:45.924
Yes, perhaps people watched her, Carla says.

00:15:45.984 --> 00:15:51.016
But the coins went into the flute player's hat.

00:15:51.076 --> 00:15:57.456
The coins were valuable, but the people in the street didn't know that.

00:15:57.516 --> 00:16:03.838
The girl and the flute player were very clever, Pete says.

00:16:03.898 --> 00:16:10.785
But now they're coming with me to the police station, the policeman says.

00:16:10.845 --> 00:16:14.959
He takes the coins from the flute player.

00:16:15.019 --> 00:16:19.252
After some time, a police car arrives.

00:16:19.312 --> 00:16:24.593
It takes the girl and the flute player to the police station.

00:16:24.653 --> 00:16:29.342
Thank you, the shopkeeper says to Carla.

00:16:29.402 --> 00:16:32.244
The coins are valuable.

00:16:32.304 --> 00:16:34.996
I want to say thank you.

00:16:35.056 --> 00:16:37.768
What can I give you?

00:16:37.828 --> 00:16:40.340
Carla smiles.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:45.324
Give Pete the stamps from South America, she says.

00:16:45.384 --> 00:16:48.967
Then he can start his stamp collecting today.

00:16:48.987 --> 00:16:53.134
Pete gives her a big smile.

00:16:53.194 --> 00:16:55.063
Thanks, Carla.

00:16:55.123 --> 00:16:57.672
He says, I love you.